-- title:
--   Places of a complex function field form a compact Riemann surface
-- statement:
--   Let $F$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbb C$-algebra structure, and assume $F$ contains an element $x$ transcendental over $\mathbb C$ such that $F$ is finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $\mathbb C(x)$ generated by $x$, and that `IsCurveOver ℂ F` holds: every nonzero $f \in F$ admits a divisor $D$ with $D(v) = \operatorname{ord}_v f$ at every place $v$ and $\deg D = 0$, each residue field $\kappa(v)$ is a finite-dimensional $\mathbb C$-module, and $\Omega[F/\mathbb C]$ is free of rank one over $F$. Here a place $v$ of $F/\mathbb C$ is a valuation subring $\mathcal O_v \subseteq F$ containing the image of $\mathbb C$, not equal to $F$, and a principal ideal ring; $\operatorname{ord}_v$ is minus the logarithm of the associated adic valuation, and $\operatorname{evalAt} v f \in \mathbb C$ is the preimage under $\mathbb C \to \kappa(v)$ of the residue of $f$ when $f \in \mathcal O_v$, and $0$ otherwise. The assertion is that the set of places of $F/\mathbb C$ carries a topology and a charted-space structure with charts valued in $\mathbb C$ for which it is an analytic ($\omega$-smooth) manifold modelled on $\mathbb C$, compact, Hausdorff and connected, and such that for every $f \neq 0$ in $F$ and every place $v$, the function $z \mapsto \operatorname{evalAt}(\varphi_v^{-1}(z))\, f$, with $\varphi_v$ the extended chart at $v$, is meromorphic at $\varphi_v(v)$ with meromorphic order there equal to $\operatorname{ord}_v f$ in $\mathbb Z \subseteq \mathbb Z \cup \{\infty\}$.
--
--   This is the analytic half of the Riemann existence correspondence: the places of a function field in one variable over $\mathbb C$ are realised as a compact Riemann surface on which the elements of the field become meromorphic functions whose analytic orders are the algebraic valuations. It is the input for the analytic study of divisors on such a field, being used for the description of $\operatorname{Pic}^0$ as a quotient by a lattice, for the differential-theoretic statements about lifts to a free algebra, and for divisibility properties of differences of point classes.
